When you run:

sudo yum update


It:

Connects to your system’s configured YUM repositories.

Checks for newer versions of all installed packages.

Downloads and installs updates (security patches, bug fixes, etc.).

Keeps your system secure and up to date.


Command	Purpose
sudo yum check-update	      Checks for available updates without installing them
sudo yum update -y	        Automatically installs all updates without prompting
sudo yum upgrade	          Similar to update, but can also remove obsolete packages


sudo apt update

sudo apt update	Refreshes package lists (metadata)
sudo apt upgrade	Installs available package updates
sudo apt full-upgrade	Performs a deeper upgrade (can remove old packages)
sudo apt install <package>	Installs a specific package
sudo apt remove <package>	Uninstalls a package


Explain the difference between the Linux kernel and a Linux distribution in your own words.

1. The Linux Kernel

The kernel is the core part of the operating system.

It’s responsible for managing:

Hardware (CPU, memory, disks, network)

Processes (running programs)

System calls (communication between software and hardware)

Think of it as the engine of a car — it makes everything run, but it’s not the whole car.

Example:
The Linux kernel handles things like:

Allocating memory to programs

Managing files on disk

Handling device drivers

Scheduling tasks for the CPU

You can’t use the kernel alone easily — it doesn’t have user interfaces, tools, or applications.

💻 2. A Linux Distribution (Distro)

A Linux distribution is a complete operating system built around the Linux kernel.

It adds all the things you need to actually use the system:

Package manager (like apt, yum, or dnf)

System utilities and libraries

Desktop environment or command-line tools

Preinstalled software (like browsers, editors, servers)

So, a distribution = Linux kernel + software + configuration + package manager

Examples:

Ubuntu

Fedora

Debian

Amazon Linux

Arch Linux

Each distribution uses the same Linux kernel base, but customizes it for different use cases and user experiences.

⚙️ In short:
Concept	Description	Analogy
Linux Kernel	The core engine that interacts with hardware and manages resources	The engine of a car
Linux Distribution	A complete operating system built around the kernel with user tools and apps	The whole car (engine + body + dashboard + controls)


What is the role of the GNU Project in the Linux ecosystem?

The GNU Project created the core utilities, libraries, and free software philosophy that make Linux systems functional and open.
The Linux kernel filled in the missing piece — together they form the GNU/Linux operating system we use today.



nano hello.sh

#!/bin/bash
echo "Hello, Linux World!"
echo "My current directory is: $(pwd)"
echo "My distribution is:"
cat /etc/os-release | grep PRETTY_NAME

What does each line do? 

Line-by-line explanation
1. #!/bin/bash

This is called a shebang (or hashbang).

It tells the system which interpreter to use to run the script.

In this case: /bin/bash, meaning the script should be executed using the Bash shell.

🧠 Essentially: “Run this script using Bash.”

2. echo "Hello, Linux World!"

The echo command prints text to the terminal.

This line displays a friendly message:

Hello, Linux World!

3. echo "My current directory is: $(pwd)"

pwd means Print Working Directory — it outputs your current directory path.

The syntax $(pwd) runs the pwd command inside the echo statement and inserts its output.

So this line prints something like:

My current directory is: /home/ec2-user

4. echo "My distribution is:"

Simply prints the label text "My distribution is:".

It’s just a heading for the next line, which shows which Linux distribution you’re running.

5. cat /etc/os-release | grep PRETTY_NAME

/etc/os-release is a system file that contains information about your Linux distribution, such as its name and version.

cat displays the contents of that file.

The | grep PRETTY_NAME part filters the output to show only the line that contains the PRETTY_NAME field.

Example output:

PRETTY_NAME="Amazon Linux 2"


So this tells you the name of your Linux distribution.


Task: Run uname -r to check the kernel version on either instance. Compare it to the latest stable version on kernel.org. Why might cloud providers like AWS use older kernels?
Hint: Consider stability, compatibility with cloud tools, and enterprise support.

[ec2-user@ip-172-31-29-16 ~]$ uname -r
6.1.153-175.280.amzn2023.x86_64

ubuntu@ip-172-31-28-147:~$ uname -r
6.14.0-1011-aws

Our instances’ kernel versions (6.1.153-… and 6.14.0-…) are behind or customized compared to the official latest stable (6.15.2 as of mid-2025) on kernel.org.

That’s normal and expected for production cloud environments.

The reason is largely about stability, compatibility, maintainability, and risk mitigation, not about being “lazy” or “behind.”
